  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper ridging continues to build into the Plains and upper\nmidwest through tonight and tomorrow. A few weak embedded vorts\nwith some mid level moisture will continue to move across the\narea on top of the ridge, but not expecting more than isolated\nsprinkles. Most model solutions show a stronger shortwave \nriding over the top of the ridge Wednesday night into Thursday,\nwith some amplification as it digs into MN. This should help\npush a surface trough/weak frontal boundary into the forecast\narea. The upper ridge builds back into the region for the\nweekend, with above average temperatures continuing. Southwest\nto westerly flow aloft seems to be the consensus from the\nensemble members as we head into next week, which could bring\na more active pattern along with more seasonable temperatures.\n\n...Heat tomorrow...\n\nThe warming trend that started today will continue into tomorrow\nas heights continue to rise. Even with some smoke aloft, most\nlocations except the far northeastern counties should get to 90\nor above tomorrow. With not much change in dew points that are\ncurrently in the 60s and low 70s, heat index values in the\nnorthern Red River Valley should get over 100 degrees. A bit\ndrier and could be slightly below 100 in the central and \nsouthern Red River Valley, but HREF probabilities of getting\nabove 100 index are over 60 percent in the Fargo area. Wet bulb\nglobe temperatures are also above 80 degrees and in the extreme\ncategory for portions of the Red River Valley. Went ahead and\nput out a heat advisory for most of the Red River Valley\ntomorrow afternoon. \n\n...Marginal severe risk on Thursday...\n\nMany of the CAMs break out some convection along the MT/ND\nborder Wednesday afternoon, then move it east Wednesday night.\nThere is a low chance that some of the cells will be holding\ntogether by the time they reach far western Benson county early\nThursday morning, but many of the model runs just have the\nconvective complex decreasing in intensity. Whether we get any\nconvective redevelopment in the afternoon along the trough axis \nwill depend on how exactly the morning plays out. At this point,\nthe 50th percentile has 1000-2500 J/kg of CAPE over all but our\nfar northwestern counties Thursday afternoon, although deep\nlayer shear is fairly mid, around 25 to 30 kts at most. Still,\ncan't rule out some isolated severe cells so will continue with\na marginal risk for Thursday.",
